\date{2025-11-09T22:25:02Z}
\title{Typing rules for functions}

\import{swarm-macros}

\p{The typing rule for lambdas is straightforward:

\rules{
  \inferrule
    {\swinfer{\Gamma, x : \tau_1}{e}{\tau_2}{\Delta}}
    {\swinfer{\Gamma}{\lamt{x}{\tau_1}{e}}{\Fun{\tau_1}{\Delta}{\tau_2}}{\varnothing}}
}

Notice how the requirements for evaluating the body show up over the arrow in the result type; we do not evaluate under lambdas, but defer evaluation of the body until evaluating an application to an argument.
}

\p{Here is the typing rule for function application:

\rules{
  \inferrule
    {\swinfer{\Gamma}{f}{\Fun{\tau_1}{\Delta_1}{\tau_2}}{\Delta_2} \\
     \swcheck{\Gamma}{e}{\tau_1}{\Delta_3}
    }
    {\swinfer{\Gamma}{\app{f}{e}}{\tau_2}{\Delta_1 \cup \Delta_2 \cup \Delta_3}}
}

Here, we infer the type of the function #{f} and check that the argument #{e} has the right type.  The capability context needed to evaluate the application #{\app{f}{e}} is the union of all the capabilities needed to evaluate #{f}, to evaluate #{e}, and to evaluate the body of the function represented by #{f}.

}